== THE SEVENTH STAGE

Well, the time drew on when the pilgrims must go on their way; so they prepared for their journey. They sent for their friends; they conferred with them; they set some time apart to commit each other to the protection of their Prince. Again there were some who brought them such things as they had, that were fit for the weak and the strong, for the women and the men, and so loaded them with such things as were necessary. Acts 28:10. Then they set forward on their way; and their friends accompanying them as far as was convenient, they again committed each other to the protection of their King, and parted.

So those who were of the pilgrims’ company went on, and Mr. Great-Heart went in front of them. Now, because the women and children were weak, they were forced to go only as fast as they could bear; by which means Mr. Ready-to-halt and Mr. Feeble-mind had more to sympathize with their condition.

When they had gone from the townsmen, and when their friends had bid them farewell, they quickly came to the place where Faithful was put to death. So they stopped there, and thanked him who had enabled Faithful to bear his cross so well; and all the more because they now found that they had a benefit from such a manly suffering as his was.

After this they went on a good way further, talking of Christian and Faithful, and how Hopeful joined himself to Christian after Faithful was dead.

Now they had come up to the hill Lucre, where the silver mine was which took Demas off from his pilgrimage, and into which, as some think, By-ends fell and perished; so they considered that. But when they had come to the old monument that stood opposite the hill Lucre, that is, to the pillar of salt, that stood also within view of Sodom and its stinking lake, they marveled, as Christian did before, that men of such knowledge and ripeness of understanding as they were should be so blinded as to turn aside here. Only they considered again that nature is not moved by the harms that others have met with, especially if the thing they look upon has an attracting power upon the foolish eye.

I saw now that they went on until they came to the river that was on this side of the Delectable Mountains; to the river where the fine trees grow on both sides, and whose leaves, if taken inwardly, are good against surfeits; where the meadows are green all the year long, and where they might lie down safely. Ps 23:2.

By this riverside, in the meadows, there were cotes and folds for sheep, a house built for the nourishing and bringing up of those lambs, the babes of those women who go on pilgrimage. Also there was one here who was entrusted with them, who could have compassion, and who could gather these lambs with his arm, and carry them in his bosom, and gently lead those that were with young. Heb 5:2; Isa 40:11. Now, to the care of this man Christiana urged her four daughters to commit their little ones, so that by these waters they might be housed, sheltered, succored, and nourished, and that none of them might be lacking in time to come. If any of them go astray, or are lost, this man will bring them back; he will also bind up what was broken, and will strengthen those who are sick. Jer 23:4; Ezek 34:11–16. Here they will never lack food, drink, and clothing; here they will be kept from thieves and robbers; for this man will die before one of those committed to his trust shall be lost. Besides, here they shall be sure to have good nurture and admonition, and shall be taught to walk in right paths, and that, you know, is a favor of no small account. Also here, as you see, are delightful waters, pleasant meadows, dainty flowers, a variety of trees, and such as bear wholesome fruit: fruit not like what Matthew ate, that fell over the wall out of Beelzebub’s garden, but fruit that brings health where there is none, and that continues and increases it where it is. So they were content to commit their little ones to him; and what also encouraged them to do so was that all this was to be at the King’s expense, and so was like a charitable home for young children and orphans.

Now they went on. And when they had come to By-path Meadow, to the stile over which Christian went with his fellow Hopeful, when they were taken by Giant Despair and put into Doubting Castle, they sat down and consulted what was best to be done: that is, whether, now that they were so strong, and had such a man as Mr. Great-Heart for their conductor, they had not better make an attempt upon the giant, demolish his castle, and, if there were any pilgrims in it, set them at liberty before they went any further. So one said one thing, and another said the contrary. One questioned whether it was lawful to go upon unconsecrated ground; another said they might, provided their end was good; but Mr. Great-Heart said, Though that last assertion cannot be universally true, yet I have a commandment to resist sin, to overcome evil, to fight the good fight of faith: and I ask you, with whom should I fight this good fight, if not with Giant Despair? I will therefore attempt the taking away of his life, and the demolishing of Doubting Castle. Then said he, Who will go with me? Then said old Honest, I will. And so will we too, said Christiana’s four sons, Matthew, Samuel, Joseph, and James; for they were young men and strong. 1 John 2:13, 14. So they left the women in the road, and with them Mr. Feeble-mind, and Mr. Ready-to-halt with his crutches, to be their guard until they came back; for in that place, though Giant Despair lived so near, as long as they kept in the road a little child might lead them. Isa 11:6.

So Mr. Great-Heart, old Honest, and the four young men went to go up to Doubting Castle, to look for Giant Despair. When they came to the castle gate, they knocked for entrance with an unusual noise. At that the old Giant comes to the gate, and Diffidence his wife follows. Then said he, Who and what is he who is so bold as to molest the Giant Despair in this way? Mr. Great-Heart replied, It is I, Great-Heart, one of the King of the Celestial country’s conductors of pilgrims to their place; and I demand of you that you open your gates for my entrance; prepare yourself also to fight, for I have come to take away your head, and to demolish Doubting Castle.

Now Giant Despair, because he was a giant, thought no man could overcome him; and again he thought, Since before now I have made a conquest of angels, shall Great-Heart make me afraid? So he armed himself, and went out. He had a cap of steel upon his head, a breastplate of fire fastened about him, and he came out in iron shoes, with a great club in his hand. Then these six men closed in on him, and beset him behind and before; also, when Diffidence the giantess came up to help him, old Mr. Honest cut her down at one blow. Then they fought for their lives, and Giant Despair was brought down to the ground, but was very loath to die. He struggled hard, and had, as they say, as many lives as a cat; but Great-Heart was his death, for he did not leave him until he had severed his head from his shoulders.

Then they set about demolishing Doubting Castle, and that, you know, could easily be done, since Giant Despair was dead. They were seven days in destroying it; and of pilgrims in it they found one Mr. Despondency, almost starved to death, and one Much-afraid, his daughter: these two they saved alive. But it would have made you wonder to see the dead bodies that lay here and there in the castle yard, and how full of dead men’s bones the dungeon was.

When Mr. Great-Heart and his companions had performed this exploit, they took Mr. Despondency and his daughter Much-afraid into their protection; for they were honest people, though they were prisoners in Doubting Castle to that tyrant Giant Despair. They, therefore, I say, took with them the head of the giant (for they had buried his body under a heap of stones), and came down to the road and to their companions, and showed them what they had done. Now, when Feeble-mind and Ready-to-halt saw that it was the head of Giant Despair indeed, they were very jocund and merry. Now Christiana, if need was, could play the viol, and her daughter Mercy the lute; so, since they were so merrily disposed, she played them a tune, and Ready-to-halt would dance. So he took Despondency’s daughter, Much-afraid, by the hand, and they fell to dancing in the road. True, he could not dance without one crutch in his hand, but I assure you he footed it well; also the girl was to be commended, for she answered the music handsomely.

As for Mr. Despondency, the music was not so much to his liking; he was for feeding rather than dancing, since he was almost starved. So Christiana gave him some of her bottle of spirits for immediate relief, and then prepared him something to eat; and in a little time the old gentleman came to himself, and began to be finely revived.

Now I saw in my dream, when all these things were finished, that Mr. Great-Heart took the head of Giant Despair, and set it upon a pole by the highway side, right opposite the pillar that Christian set up as a caution to pilgrims who came after, to take heed of entering into his grounds.

Then he wrote under it, upon a marble stone, these verses following:

#quote[“This is the head of him whose name only \
In former times did pilgrims terrify. \
His castle’s down, and Diffidence his wife \
Brave Mr. Great-Heart has bereft of life. \
Despondency, his daughter Much-afraid, \
Great-Heart for them also the man has play’d. \
Who hereof doubts, if he’ll but cast his eye \
Up hither, may his scruples satisfy. \
This head also, when doubting cripples dance, \
Doth show from fears they have deliverance.”]

When these men had thus bravely shown themselves against Doubting Castle, and had slain Giant Despair, they went forward, and went on until they came to the Delectable Mountains, where Christian and Hopeful refreshed themselves with the varieties of the place. They also acquainted themselves with the shepherds there, who welcomed them, as they had done Christian before, to the Delectable Mountains.

Now the shepherds, seeing so great a train follow Mr. Great-Heart (for they were well acquainted with him), said to him, Good sir, you have got a goodly company here; please, where did you find all these?

Then Mr. Great-Heart replied,

#quote[“First, here is Christiana and her train, \
Her sons, and her sons’ wives, who, like the wain, \
Keep by the pole, and do by compass steer \
From sin to grace, else they had not been here. \
Next here’s old Honest come on pilgrimage, \
Ready-to-halt too, who I dare engage \
True-hearted is, and so is Feeble-mind, \
Who willing was not to be left behind. \
Despondency, good man, is coming after, \
And so also is Much-afraid, his daughter. \
May we have entertainment here, or must \
We further go? Let’s know whereon to trust.”]

Then said the shepherds, This is a comforting company. You are welcome to us; for we have for the feeble as well as for the strong. Our Prince has an eye to what is done to the least of these; therefore Infirmity must not be a block to our entertainment. Matt 25:40. So they took them to the palace door, and then said to them, Come in, Mr. Feeble-Mind; come in, Mr. Ready-to-halt; come in, Mr. Despondency, and Mrs. Much-afraid his daughter. These, Mr. Great-Heart, said the shepherds to the guide, we call in by name, because they are the most apt to draw back; but as for you, and the rest who are strong, we leave you to your usual liberty. Then said Mr. Great-Heart, This day I see that grace does shine in your faces, and that you are my Lord’s shepherds indeed; for you have not pushed these diseased ones with either side or shoulder, but have rather strewn their way into the palace with flowers, as you should. Ezek 34:21.

So the feeble and weak went in, and Mr. Great-Heart and the rest followed. When they had also sat down, the shepherds said to those of the weaker sort, What is it that you would like? For, said they, all things must be managed here for the support of the weak, as well as for the warning of the unruly. So they made them a feast of things easy to digest, and that were pleasant to the palate and nourishing; and when they had received it, they went to their rest, each one to his own place.

When morning had come, because the mountains were high and the day clear, and because it was the custom of the shepherds to show the pilgrims some rarities before their departure, therefore, after they were ready, and had refreshed themselves, the shepherds took them out into the fields, and showed them first what they had shown to Christian before.

Then they took them to some new places. The first was Mount Marvel, where they looked, and saw a man at a distance who tumbled the hills about with words. Then they asked the shepherds what that meant. So they told them that that man was the son of one Mr. Great-grace, of whom you read in the first part of the records of the Pilgrim’s Progress; and he is set there to teach pilgrims how to believe down, or to tumble out of their ways, whatever difficulties they should meet with, by faith. Mark 11:23, 24. Then said Mr. Great-Heart, I know him; he is a man above many.

Then they took them to another place, called Mount Innocence. And there they saw a man clothed all in white; and two men, Prejudice and Ill-will, continually casting dirt upon him. Now look: the dirt, whatever they cast at him, would in a little time fall off again, and his garment would look as clean as if no dirt had been cast at it. Then said the pilgrims, What does this mean? The shepherds answered, This man is named Godlyman, and this garment is to show the innocence of his life. Now, those who throw dirt at him are such as hate his well-doing; but, as you see the dirt will not stick upon his clothes, so it shall be with him who lives innocently in the world. Whoever they are who would make such men dirty, they labor all in vain; for God, when a little time has passed, will cause their innocence to break forth as the light, and their righteousness as the noonday.

Then they took them to Mount Charity, where they showed them a man who had a bundle of cloth lying before him, out of which he cut coats and garments for the poor who stood about him; yet his bundle or roll of cloth was never the less. Then said they, What does this mean? This is, said the shepherds, to show you that he who has a heart to give of his labor to the poor shall never lack the means to do it. He who waters shall be watered himself. And the cake that the widow gave to the prophet did not make her have any less in her barrel.

They took them also to the place where they saw one Fool and one Want-wit washing an Ethiopian, intending to make him white; but the more they washed him, the blacker he was. Then they asked the shepherds what that meant. So they told them, saying, Thus it is with the vile person; all means used to get such a one a good name shall in the end only make him more abominable. Thus it was with the pharisees; and so it shall be with all hypocrites.

Then said Mercy, the wife of Matthew, to Christiana her mother, Mother, I would like, if it may be, to see the hole in the hill, or what is commonly called the By-way to hell. So her mother made her wish known to the shepherds. Then they went to the door; it was on the side of a hill; and they opened it, and told Mercy to listen a while. So she listened, and heard one saying, Cursed be my father for holding my feet back from the way of peace and life. Another said, Oh, that I had been torn in pieces before I had, to save my life, lost my soul! And another said, If I were to live again, how I would deny myself, rather than come to this place! Then it was as if the very earth groaned and quaked under the feet of this young woman for fear; so she turned white, and came away trembling, saying, Blessed be he and she who is delivered from this place!

Now, when the shepherds had shown them all these things, they took them back to the palace, and entertained them with what the house would afford. But Mercy, being a young and married woman, longed for something that she saw there, but was ashamed to ask. Her mother-in-law then asked her what ailed her, for she looked like one not well. Then said Mercy, There is a looking-glass hanging up in the dining room that I cannot take my mind off; if, therefore, I do not have it, I think I shall miscarry. Then said her mother, I will mention your wish to the shepherds, and they will not deny you. But she said, I am ashamed that these men should know that I longed. No, my daughter, said she, it is no shame, but a virtue, to long for such a thing as that. So Mercy said, Then, mother, if you please, ask the shepherds if they are willing to sell it.

Now the glass was one in a thousand. Held one way, it would show a man his own features exactly; and turn it but another way, and it would show one the very face and likeness of the Prince of pilgrims himself. Yes, I have talked with those who can tell, and they have said that they have seen the very crown of thorns upon his head by looking in that glass; they have also seen in it the holes in his hands, his feet, and his side. Yes, there is such an excellence in this glass that it will show him to one wherever they have a mind to see him, whether living or dead; whether on earth or in heaven; whether in a state of humiliation or in his exaltation; whether coming to suffer or coming to reign. Jas 1:23; 1 Cor 13:12; 2 Cor 3:18.

Christiana therefore went to the shepherds privately (now the names of the shepherds were Knowledge, Experience, Watchful, and Sincere), and said to them, There is one of my daughters, a woman with child, who I think longs for something that she has seen in this house; and she thinks that she shall miscarry if you should deny her.

#speech[#smallcaps[Experience]: Call her, call her; she shall certainly have whatever we can help her to. So they called her, and said to her, Mercy, what is that thing you would have? Then she blushed, and said, The great glass that hangs up in the dining room. So Sincere ran and fetched it, and with a joyful consent it was given her. Then she bowed her head, and gave thanks, and said, By this I know that I have obtained favor in your eyes.]

They also gave the other young women such things as they desired, and gave their husbands great commendations, because they had joined with Mr. Great-Heart in the slaying of Giant Despair and the demolishing of Doubting Castle.

About Christiana’s neck the shepherds put a necklace, and so they did about the necks of her four daughters; also they put earrings in their ears, and jewels on their foreheads.

When they had a mind to go on from there, the shepherds let them go in peace, but did not give them those particular cautions which before were given to Christian and his companion. The reason was that these had Great-Heart for their guide, who was one well acquainted with things, and so could give them their cautions more seasonably, that is, just when the danger was drawing near. What cautions Christian and his companion had received from the shepherds, they had also lost by the time they needed to put them in practice. So here was the advantage that this company had over the other.

From there they went on singing, and they said,

#quote[“Behold how fitly are the stages set \
For their relief that pilgrims are become, \
And how they us receive without one let, \
That make the other life our mark and home!]

#quote[What novelties they have to us they give, \
That we, though pilgrims, joyful lives may live; \
They do upon us, too, such things bestow, \
That show we pilgrims are, where’er we go.”]
